{"qid":"Q41137","names":["Assyria"],"title":"Assyria","endonym":null,"capsule":"Assyria in this entry covers the Old and Middle Assyrian periods, from the city-state of Ashur's emergence as a major polity (attested here from 1800 BC) through the Middle Assyrian Empire under Tukulti-Ninurta I (r. c. 1244–1208 BC) and Tiglath-Pileser I (r. c. 1114–1076 BC) to the end of the dataset span at 901 BC, after which the Neo-Assyrian period begins. The Middle Assyrian kingdom under Adad-nirari I (r. c. 1307–1275 BC) destroyed the rival Mitanni state and established Assyrian dominance across upper Mesopotamia. A period of weakness from c. 1050 to 911 BC preceded the Neo-Assyrian revival, during which Assyrian control contracted sharply under pressure from Aramaean migrations.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Old and Middle Assyrian territorial extents are partially attested through cuneiform administrative records (Ashur archives, Kultepe tablets) and treaty texts, but precise frontiers, especially during periods of expansion and contraction, are largely reconstructed from archaeological and textual evidence.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Assyria","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Assyria"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Assyria","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assyria"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Assyria","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Tudiya","nativeName":"𒌅𒁲𒅀","roman":"Tudiya","start":-2450,"end":-2400,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Adamu","nativeName":"𒀭𒀭𒈬","roman":"Adamu","start":-2400,"end":-2380,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageL